{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Field-Based Transfer and Coaching","title":"Revising Plans After Field Evidence","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting revising plans after field evidence to field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"06","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_revising_plans_after_field_evidence","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/07/05"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_7_6_revising_plans_after_field_evidence_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_7_6_revising_plans_after_field_evidence_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_7_6_revising_plans_after_field_evidence_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_7_6_revising_plans_after_field_evidence_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Revising Plans After Field Evidence\n\n![Revising Plans After Field Evidence textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/07-06-revising-plans-after-field-evidence.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nWhen a lesson underperforms, two unhelpful instincts compete: defend the plan (\"the design is sound, the students just didn't do their part\") or scrap it entirely (\"flipped learning doesn't work\"). Both skip the diagnostic step that distinguishes a teacher who learns from one who guesses. Field evidence rarely condemns a whole plan; it usually points at one component. The flipped lesson did not fail because flipping is wrong; it failed because the at-home prep step assumed home access that 17 of 26 students did not have. Revision is the work of isolating the broken component and changing it while keeping what worked.\n\nThis section treats revision as evidence-driven diagnosis. The skill is reading field data to locate the specific cause, then making the minimal change that fixes it, with follow-up data to confirm the fix.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Read field evidence to isolate which component of a plan failed, rather than defending or scrapping the whole.\n- Distinguish a design flaw from a context mismatch (laziness versus an access barrier).\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move, including an AI reframing you rejected because it ignored the real cause.\n- Show evidence-based revision: original plan, prompting data, targeted change, and follow-up data.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA plan is not one decision; it is a chain of them. A flipped lesson chains an at-home video to an in-class activity. When the lesson disappoints, the question is which link broke, and the data usually answers it precisely. If the in-class AI-coached set worked for the nine students who watched the video but the other seventeen never watched, the broken link is the at-home prep, not the in-class design.\n\nThe mistake is reasoning about the whole when the data is about a part. \"The flipped model failed\" throws away a component that worked for everyone who reached it. The evidence points narrower than the conclusion you are tempted to draw, and revision means following the evidence to the actual link, not the whole chain.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEvidence-based revision is component diagnosis. You decompose the plan into its sequential links, attach the field data to each link, and find the one where the data shows the failure. Then you ask a second question the diagnosis depends on: is this link broken by a design flaw (the activity itself is poorly built) or by a context mismatch (the activity assumes a resource the students do not have)? The answer determines the fix. A design flaw needs a redesigned activity; a context mismatch needs the same activity moved to where the resource exists.\n\nIn the flipped case, the failing link is at-home prep, and the cause is a context mismatch: an exit question shows most non-watchers cited unreliable home time, not unwillingness. That diagnosis points to a precise fix, move the video to a seven-minute in-class viewing, which keeps the AI-coached set that already worked and relocates only the link that broke. The follow-up data (24 of 26 reaching the set, up from 9) confirms the diagnosis was right.\n\nThree lenses sharpen the diagnosis:\n\n- **Critical edtech studies** (Selwyn, 2016) distinguishes \"students didn't do it\" from \"students couldn't do it.\" Reading non-completion as laziness blames the learner; reading it as an access barrier locates the fixable cause in the design.\n- **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** warns that AI revision tools propose engagement gimmicks (a points system) that change the surface without touching the diagnosed cause. A fix that ignores the cause is not a fix.\n- **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** asks whether the technology component or the pedagogical sequence is the failing link, since the same low-completion symptom can come from either.\n\n> **Recent evidence (the homework gap):** The access barrier in this case is a documented national pattern, not a one-classroom anomaly. Roughly 15% of U.S. households with school-age children lack a high-speed home connection; about 12% of teens report being unable to complete homework because they lack reliable computer or internet access, and 22% sometimes do homework on a cellphone. The gap is sharply income-stratified: about 44% of households earning under $30,000 lack high-speed home internet versus about 6% of households earning over $75,000 ([NCES, Children's Internet Access at Home](https://nces.ed.gov/programs/coe/indicator/cch/home-internet-access); [Pew Research Center](https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2020/03/16/as-schools-close-due-to-the-coronavirus-some-u-s-students-face-a-digital-homework-gap/)). Non-completion is frequently \"couldn't do it\" (access), not \"didn't do it\" (effort), which is exactly the context mismatch this section teaches you to diagnose.\n\nA lens has done its work when it changed which component you revised, not just how you described the failure.\n\n<inline-check question=\"17 of 26 students did not watch the at-home video; an exit question shows most cited unreliable home time. Is this a design flaw or a context mismatch?\" explanation=\"A context mismatch: the at-home prep activity assumes a resource (reliable home time) that the students do not have. The cure is to move the same activity to where the resource exists, an in-class viewing, not to redesign the activity itself. Reading non-completion as unwillingness would have misclassified it as a discipline problem and fixed nothing.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A context mismatch: the activity assumes a resource the students lack\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A design flaw: the video activity itself is poorly built\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Neither: the students simply chose not to do the work\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare two responses to the same disappointing result. The defend-on-principle version protects the whole plan:\n\n> Keep the flipped design; reassign the video and warn students to watch it.\n\nThis treats the symptom as a discipline problem and changes nothing structural. The diagnostic version isolates the link:\n\n> 17 of 26 students did not watch at home, and an exit question showed most cited unreliable home time, not unwillingness. The in-class AI coaching worked for the 9 who watched. So the failing link is at-home prep, not the design. Move the video to a 7-minute in-class viewing before the AI-coached set.\n\nThe second version reads the data to a component, names the cause (access, not laziness), and changes only the broken link. The follow-up confirms it: 24 of 26 reached the problem set, versus 9 before. The AI tool's suggestion to add a gamified points system was rejected, because points address motivation and the diagnosed cause was access.\n\nModel the broken link directly. The reach to the in-class AI-coached set is the students who watched at home, plus, if you relocate the prep into class, the rest of the class who can then watch. Set the prep location to 0 (at home) or 1 (moved in-class) and watch reach climb as you lower home access.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Reach to the AI-coached set: where the prep video lives\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"class_size\",\"label\":\"Class size\",\"min\":20,\"max\":30,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"students\",\"default\":26},{\"name\":\"home_access_pct\",\"label\":\"Students with reliable home time\",\"min\":0,\"max\":100,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":35},{\"name\":\"prep_in_class\",\"label\":\"Prep moved in-class (0 = at home, 1 = in-class)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":1,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Students reaching the AI-coached set\",\"unit\":\"students\",\"expr\":\"class_size * home_access_pct / 100 + prep_in_class * (class_size - class_size * home_access_pct / 100)\"},{\"label\":\"Students locked out of the core activity\",\"unit\":\"students\",\"expr\":\"(1 - prep_in_class) * (class_size - class_size * home_access_pct / 100)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"home_access_pct\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n### Annotated Judgment Call: Revising a Flipped Lesson After the Data\n\nAisha planned a flipped lesson: students watch a video at home, then use class time for an AI-coached problem set. After teaching it once, only 9 of 26 students had watched the video.\n\n1. **Before (plan defended on principle):** \"Keep the flipped design; reassign the video and warn students to watch it.\"\n2. **Field evidence read:** 17 of 26 did not watch at home. An exit question showed most cited unreliable home time, an access barrier, not laziness.\n3. **Cause isolated:** The in-class AI coaching worked for the 9 who had watched. The failing part was the at-home prep, so Aisha revises the cause rather than scrapping the whole plan.\n4. **AI suggestion held to the evidence:** An AI tool proposed a gamified points system but never addressed home access. She rejected that reframing and kept her focus on the access barrier.\n5. **After (evidence-based revision):** Move the video to a 7-minute in-class viewing before the AI-coached set. Follow-up data: 24 of 26 students reached the problem set, versus 9 before.\n\nThe record shows the original plan, the prompting data, the change, and the follow-up data, not a hunch that it \"felt better.\"\n\nNow run the diagnosis under pressure. Each turn either isolates the broken link or overcorrects.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Diagnosing a disappointing flipped lesson\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"Only 9 of 26 students watched the at-home video before your AI-coached problem set. Your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Conclude the flipped model failed and scrap it\",\"next\":\"scrap\",\"feedback\":\"You reasoned about the whole when the data is about a part.\"},{\"label\":\"Reassign the video and warn students to watch it next time\",\"next\":\"defend\",\"feedback\":\"You treated a symptom as a discipline problem and changed nothing structural.\"},{\"label\":\"Read the data to a specific link before changing anything\",\"next\":\"read\",\"feedback\":\"You located the failing component first.\"}]},\"scrap\":{\"prompt\":\"Scrapping discards the in-class AI coaching that worked for the 9 who watched. What did you lose?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A component that worked for everyone who reached it\",\"next\":\"restart\",\"feedback\":\"Overcorrecting throws away the links the data exonerated.\"}]},\"defend\":{\"prompt\":\"You blamed the students. An exit question shows most non-watchers cited unreliable home time, not unwillingness. Reconsider.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reread the data as an access barrier, not laziness\",\"next\":\"read\",\"feedback\":\"You corrected a misclassification of the cause.\"}]},\"restart\":{\"prompt\":\"Restart and read the data to the link first.\",\"feedback\":\"The evidence points narrower than the conclusion you were tempted to draw.\",\"choices\":[]},\"read\":{\"prompt\":\"The in-class coaching worked for the 9 who watched; 17 non-watchers cited unreliable home time. Which link is broken and what is the cause?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"At-home prep, broken by a context mismatch (no reliable home time)\",\"next\":\"fix\",\"feedback\":\"You isolated the link and classified the cause correctly.\"},{\"label\":\"The in-class design, broken by a design flaw\",\"next\":\"restart\",\"feedback\":\"The data exonerates the in-class set; it worked for everyone who reached it.\"}]},\"fix\":{\"prompt\":\"An AI tool suggests adding a gamified points system. Do you adopt it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; points address motivation, but the diagnosed cause is access\",\"next\":\"relocate\",\"feedback\":\"You held the fix to the diagnosed cause.\"},{\"label\":\"Yes; gamification boosts engagement\",\"next\":\"restart\",\"feedback\":\"A fix that ignores the cause is not a fix.\"}]},\"relocate\":{\"prompt\":\"You move the video to a 7-minute in-class viewing before the AI-coached set. Follow-up: 24 of 26 reach the set, versus 9 before.\",\"feedback\":\"You read the data to the link, classified the cause, changed only that link, and confirmed with follow-up data.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nLocate the failing component before you change anything, and confirm the cause is a flaw or a mismatch before you choose the fix. The order is: read the data to the link, classify the cause, change only that link, then gather follow-up data; this sequencing is [teacher professional judgment](https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304) applied as diagnosis rather than defense. Aisha follows it exactly; she does not touch the in-class set that worked, classifies the prep failure as access rather than effort, relocates only the video, and measures the result.\n\nBefore you call a revision evidence-based, three checks should pass:\n\n1. **Component check:** Does your data point to a specific link in the plan, or are you revising the whole because the result disappointed?\n2. **Cause check:** Did you classify the failure as a design flaw or a context mismatch, and does your fix match that classification?\n3. **Confirmation check:** What follow-up data shows the change worked, and what AI-suggested fix did you reject because it ignored the diagnosed cause?\n\nA revision with no follow-up data is a hope, not an evidence-based change.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is defending the plan and blaming the students; \"they should have watched\" reads an access barrier as a discipline problem and fixes nothing. The second is overcorrecting by scrapping a whole plan when one link failed, which discards the components that worked. The third is accepting an AI revision that addresses the wrong cause; when a tool proposes gamification for a problem the data shows is about home access, adopting it changes the surface and leaves the failure intact.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply component diagnosis to a different disappointment. You ran a station rotation where one station used an AI research assistant, and the lesson felt chaotic. The temptation is \"stations don't work for this class.\" Decompose instead. The data: the two non-tech stations ran smoothly, but the AI-assistant station had a line of confused students because the login process took four minutes per student and ate the rotation time.\n\nThe failing link is not the rotation structure; it is the login bottleneck at one station, and the cause is a design flaw (a slow setup), not a context mismatch. The fix is narrow: pre-log the devices before class or assign a station helper to manage logins, leaving the rotation intact. Follow-up data would be whether the AI station now completes within the rotation window. If an AI tool suggested abandoning stations for whole-class instruction, you reject it, because it discards two stations that worked to solve a problem confined to one. The walkthrough repeats: find the link, classify the cause, fix only that link, measure again.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a revision, keep a two-column note: **what the data shows about each link** on the left, **what changed** on the right. A revision that changed more than the data implicated is overcorrecting; a revision that changed nothing structural despite clear data is defending. The match between the diagnosed link and the change made is the mark of evidence-based revision.\n\nA strong reader of a revision can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which specific component does the field data implicate, and which components does it exonerate?\n2. Was the failure a design flaw or a context mismatch, and does the fix match?\n3. What follow-up data confirms the change worked, with what before-and-after numbers?\n4. What AI-suggested reframing was rejected because it addressed the wrong cause?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA revision artifact is strong when a reader can see the original plan, the data that prompted the change, the single component changed, and the follow-up data that confirmed it. That requires before-and-after numbers, not a claim that the lesson \"felt better.\" Polished narration is the last layer; a revision that reads persuasively but cannot point to the implicated component or show follow-up data has documented a hunch, not a diagnosis.\n\nA useful closing line sounds like this: \"I moved the video into class because the data showed home access, not effort, was the failing link, and follow-up completion rose from 9 to 24 of 26. I rejected the gamified points suggestion, because it fixed a motivation problem the data never found.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same diagnostic discipline to a success you want to scale. A lesson worked well in your fourth-period class, and you plan to run it identically in second period. Component diagnosis still applies, but now you are predicting which links depend on conditions that differ between the two classes (second period has three more multilingual learners and meets right after lunch). Rather than copying the plan whole, you identify which components rest on conditions that change and pre-adjust them. If you can diagnose a plan's components before they fail in a new context, not only after, you understand revision as forward-looking judgment rather than damage repair.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/07-06-revising-plans-after-field-evidence-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"07.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints for CAT 531 07.06. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_revising_plans_after_field_evidence\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your revision points the data at a specific component, classifies the cause as flaw or mismatch, changes only that component, and shows before-and-after follow-up data. That is what separates a diagnosis from a guess.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching\", \"cat531_supplement_revising_plans_after_field_evidence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). Mishra & Koehler (2006), https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x; AI-TPACK: Ning et al. (2024), https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- critical edtech studies. Selwyn, N. (2016). Is Technology Good for Education? Polity. ISBN 9780745696478\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017); Williamson, Macgilchrist & Potter (2023), Re-examining AI, automation and datafication in education, https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2023.2167830\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_6_revising_plans_after_field_evidence_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_revising_plans_after_field_evidence","stem":"A candidate planned a flipped lesson where students watch a video at home and use class time for an AI-coached problem set. After teaching it once, only 9 of 26 students had watched the video. Which revision is driven by the field evidence rather than by the candidate's preference?","options":["Keep the flipped plan because the design is sound in principle.","Move the video to a 7-minute in-class viewing because most students lacked reliable at-home time and the AI coaching assumed they had watched it.","Assign the same video again and warn students to watch it.","Add a quiz penalty for students who did not watch."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The field evidence (17 of 26 did not watch at home) points to an access barrier, so the defensible revision moves the video into class. Repeating the plan or adding penalties ignores the evidence.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_6_ignore_field_evidence","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_revising_plans_after_field_evidence","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_6_revising_plans_after_field_evidence_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"After one rough lesson, the candidate wants to scrap the whole AI-coached problem set. What does responsible revision require before that large a change?","options":["Scrap it immediately because one lesson felt chaotic.","Separate which part actually failed (the at-home prep) from the part that worked (in-class coaching) so she revises the cause, not the whole plan.","Replace it with a tool the candidate personally prefers.","Blame the students for not preparing and keep everything the same."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Revision after field evidence isolates the failing component instead of discarding everything. The candidate should change the cause (prep access), not abandon a part that worked.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_6_overcorrect_on_one_lesson","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_6_revising_plans_after_field_evidence_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"The candidate gives an AI tool her lesson plan and the line that only 9 students watched the video, and asks for a revision. The AI proposes a gamified points system but never addresses home access. How should she use it?","options":["Adopt the gamified system because the AI is good at engagement ideas.","Note that the AI ignored the access cause she observed, and revise toward the access barrier instead of the AI's engagement framing.","Trust that gamification will fix attendance to the video.","Ask the AI to add more game mechanics."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The AI reframed an access problem as an engagement problem. The candidate's field evidence about home access must govern the revision, not the AI's default framing.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_6_ai_reframes_the_cause","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_6_revising_plans_after_field_evidence_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"What record best shows the candidate's revision was evidence-based rather than a hunch?","options":["A note that the second lesson felt better.","The original plan, the field data that prompted the change, the specific revision, and the follow-up data showing whether participation rose.","A new, longer lesson plan.","A statement that the candidate has more experience now."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Evidence-based revision shows the original plan, the prompting data, the change, and follow-up data. A feeling that it went better is not evidence the revision worked.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_6_feeling_replaces_followup","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"686fe201adbdfe5d3f60e30f324dff1db4945ff3b1bf8bd819953a719ec07d4a","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}